<h2>The setting: an NDPS trial at the evidence stage</h2>
<p>The applicant was an undertrial in FIR No. 5/2025, registered by the Crime Branch, Delhi, under Sections 21, 25 and 29 of the NDPS Act, 1985 — possession, allowing premises to be used, and criminal conspiracy. The trial, pending before the Special Judge (NDPS) at Karkardooma Courts, had reached the stage of prosecution evidence. An earlier application for interim bail before the Special Judge had been dismissed in August 2026, bringing the applicant to the High Court under Section 483 of the BNSS.</p>

<h2>The plea and the State's response</h2>
<p>The ground was specific and time-bound: the applicant's wife suffered from a serious surgical-site infection arising from a pilonidal sinus and was scheduled for surgery on 16 October 2026. The applicant sought to be present for the operation and the immediate recovery that follows. The State resisted the length of the relief sought — thirty days — contending the surgery was not of that gravity.</p>
<p>Two features of the record proved decisive. The medical documents had been verified by the State, and the surgery date stood confirmed. With the genuineness of the emergency established, the question reduced to calibration: how much liberty, for how long, on what terms.</p>

<h2>What the Court ordered</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Ten days, not thirty</h4><p>The Court granted interim bail for ten days from release — enough to cover the surgery and immediate post-operative needs, without converting a humanitarian window into de facto regular bail.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Verification built in</h4><p>At surrender, the applicant must furnish the medical documents relating to the surgery to the jail superintendent — the relief carries its own audit trail.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>A fallback date</h4><p>If the surgery did not take place by 16 October 2026, the applicant was to surrender by 4 PM the following day. The liberty was tied to the event, not the calendar alone.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The usual architecture of conditions applied: a personal bond of ₹25,000 with one surety, no criminal activity, residence at the recorded address, a working mobile number disclosed to the jail authorities, no influence on witnesses, and no travel beyond Delhi without permission.</p>

<h2>How Delhi courts approach humanitarian interim bail</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Question the court asks</th><th>What satisfies it</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Is the emergency real?</td><td>Hospital records verified by the State or jail authorities; a confirmed date for the procedure.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Is the applicant's presence needed?</td><td>The relationship (spouse, parent, child), the nature of the procedure, and the absence of other family members to attend.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Is the window proportionate?</td><td>Days matched to the event — courts routinely trim the period sought, as the cut from thirty days to ten here shows.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Will the applicant return?</td><td>Bonds, sureties, address and phone disclosure, a fixed surrender time, and document production at surrender.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical notes for applicants</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Documents first.</strong> File the hospital papers with the application and invite verification; relief follows confirmation, not assertion.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for a realistic period.</strong> An inflated prayer invites resistance; a tailored one invites trust.</li>
    <li><strong>Expect event-linked terms.</strong> If the operation is postponed, the bail may collapse with it — build that risk into the family's planning.</li>
    <li><strong>Honour the surrender date.</strong> Default converts a humanitarian order into a cancellation ground and poisons every future application.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>Interim bail of this kind does not decide the main bail application, and the Court said nothing on the merits of the NDPS charge. What the order protects is something narrower and older than the statute: the ability of a prisoner, still presumed innocent, to stand beside his family at a moment of medical crisis — for a measured time, on measurable terms.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Outcomes in bail matters depend on the facts of each case.</p></div>
